TestCase
********

.[perex]
In simple tests, assertions can follow one after another. However, sometimes it's advantageous to wrap assertions in a test class to structure them.

The class must extend `Tester\TestCase`, and we refer to it simply as a **TestCase**. The class must contain test methods starting with `test`. These methods will be executed as tests:

```php
use Tester\Assert;

class RectangleTest extends Tester\TestCase
{
	public function testOne()
	{
		Assert::same(/* ... */);
	}

	public function testTwo()
	{
		Assert::match(/* ... */);
	}
}

# Run test methods
(new RectangleTest)->run();
```

A TestCase written this way can be further enhanced with `setUp()` and `tearDown()` methods. They are called before and after each test method, respectively:

```php
use Tester\Assert;

class NextTest extends Tester\TestCase
{
	protected function setUp()
	{
		# Preparation
	}

	protected function tearDown()
	{
		# Clean-up
	}

	public function testOne()
	{
		Assert::same(/* ... */);
	}

	public function testTwo()
	{
		Assert::match(/* ... */);
	}
}

# Run test methods
(new NextTest)->run();

/*


Method Call Order
-----------------
setUp()
testOne()
tearDown()

setUp()
testTwo()
tearDown()
*/
```

If an error occurs during the `setUp()` or `tearDown()` phase, the test will fail overall. If an error occurs in the test method itself, the `tearDown()` method is still executed, but any errors within it are suppressed.

Within a test method, you can skip the current test at any time by calling `$this->skip('reason')`, for example when a prerequisite is not met.

We recommend writing the [@testCase |test-annotations#testCase] annotation at the beginning of the test file. The command-line test runner will then execute the individual TestCase methods in separate processes and in parallel using multiple threads. This can significantly speed up the entire testing process.

/--php
<?php
/** @testCase */
\--


Method Annotations
==================

Several annotations are available for test methods to facilitate testing. Write them above the test method.

Since version 2.7, you can also write them as PHP attributes from the `Tester\Attributes` namespace. They work exactly like the annotations, but your IDE completes them and checks the class names you use in them. If a method has both, the attribute applies. The examples below show the attribute first and then the same thing as an annotation. .{data-version:2.7.0}


@throws .[filter]
-----------------
It is equivalent to using `Assert::exception()` inside the test method, but the notation is clearer:

```php
use Tester\Attributes\Throws;

#[Throws(RuntimeException::class)]
public function testOne()
{
	// ...
}


#[Throws(LogicException::class, 'Wrong argument order')]
public function testTwo()
{
	// ...
}
```

The optional second argument is a pattern that the exception message must match, just like in [Assert::exception() |assertions#Assert::exception]. Instead of an exception class, the attribute also accepts an `E_*` constant, so the test can expect a PHP error, for example a warning: `#[Throws(E_WARNING)]`.

The same written as annotations:

```php
/**
 * @throws RuntimeException
 */
public function testOne()
{
	// ...
}


/**
 * @throws LogicException  Wrong argument order
 */
public function testTwo()
{
	// ...
}
```


@dataProvider .[filter]
-----------------------
This annotation is useful when you want to run the test method multiple times with different parameters. (Do not confuse it with the annotation of the same name for [test files |test-annotations#dataProvider].)

After it, specify the name of a method that returns the arguments for the test method. This method must return an array or a Traversable object. A simple example:

```php
use Tester\Attributes\DataProvider;

public function getLoopArgs()
{
	return [
		[1, 2, 3],
		[4, 5, 6],
		[7, 8, 9],
	];
}


#[DataProvider('getLoopArgs')]
public function testLoop($a, $b, $c)
{
	// ...
}
```

The same written as an annotation:

```php
/**
 * @dataProvider getLoopArgs
 */
public function testLoop($a, $b, $c)
{
	// ...
}
```

If the provider returns no data sets at all, the test fails. An empty provider is almost always a mistake, such as an empty file or a filter that matches nothing, and a test method that silently didn't run would look as if it had passed. .{data-version:2.7.0}

The second variation of the **@dataProvider** annotation accepts a path to an INI file (relative to the test file) as a parameter. The method is called as many times as there are sections in the INI file. File `loop-args.ini`:

```ini
[one]
a=1
b=2
c=3

[two]
a=4
b=5
c=6

[three]
a=7
b=8
c=9
```

and the method that uses the INI file:

```php
#[DataProvider('loop-args.ini')]
public function testLoop($a, $b, $c)
{
	// ...
}
```

As an annotation, write `@dataProvider loop-args.ini`.

Similarly, instead of an INI file, you can reference a PHP script. It must return an array or a Traversable object. File `loop-args.php`:

```php
return [
	['a' => 1, 'b' => 2, 'c' => 3],
	['a' => 4, 'b' => 5, 'c' => 6],
	['a' => 7, 'b' => 8, 'c' => 9],
];
```

As with the [data provider for test files |test-annotations#dataProvider], you can append a filtering query after the file name to run the method only for the matching sections. With the attribute, pass the query as the second argument, for example `#[DataProvider('loop-args.ini', 'one')]`.

A method can also have several data providers, written as several attributes or annotations. It then runs with the data sets of all of them, one after another.
